{"name":"Blue Perennial","hex":"#bcd7df","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"perennial","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Lasting or remaining active throughout the year, for multiple years, or all the time.","Continuing without cessation or intermission for several years, or for an undetermined or infinite period; never-ending or never failing; perpetual, unceasing."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant that is active throughout the year, or has a life cycle of more than two growing seasons.","A thing that lasts forever.\nA person or thing (such as a problem) that appears or returns regularly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/perennial"}],"entries":[{"title":"Perennial","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, the term perennial is used to differentiate a plant from shorter-lived annuals and biennials. It has thus been defined as a plant that lives more than 2 years. The term is also loosely used to distinguish plants with little or no woody growth from trees and shrubs, which are also technically perennials. An estimated 94% of plant species are perennials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Cykoria_podroznik_pokroj.jpg/330px-Cykoria_podroznik_pokroj.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perennial"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/RIMG0163uf.JPG/500px-RIMG0163uf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/RIMG0163uf.JPG/1280px-RIMG0163uf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/RIMG0163uf.JPG/500px-RIMG0163uf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dahlia plants are tender perennials that originate from climates that are warm all year round and need special care to survive cold winters.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARIMG0163uf.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Switchgrass_roots.jpg/500px-Switchgrass_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Switchgrass_roots.jpg/1280px-Switchgrass_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Switchgrass_roots.jpg/500px-Switchgrass_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars